 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Every word has one strong syllable",
     "texto": [
      "When you say a word, one **syllable** sounds stronger: it is the **stressed syllable**.\n\n- **CA**-sa · **ME**-sa · pa-**LA**-bra\n- ca-**FÉ** · can-**CIÓN** · **MÚ**-si-ca\n\nSometimes the strong syllable is marked with an **accent** (´). The accent is a clue: it tells you where to put the stress when you read."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "When do you write the accent?",
     "texto": [
      "There are two basic rules:\n\n- If the word ends in a **vowel, n or s**, the stress goes on the **second-to-last** syllable: **CA**-sa, **JO**-ven.\n- If it ends in **another consonant**, the stress goes on the **last** one: pa-**PEL**, re-**LOJ**.\n\nWhen a word breaks these rules, it gets an **accent**: ca-**FÉ**, can-**CIÓN**, **ÁR**-bol. And if the stress comes before the second-to-last syllable, it always has one: **MÚ**-si-ca."
     ],
